// Signing for the SipAI target.
//
// A fresh clone signs to run locally — ad hoc, no Apple account, no
// edits — so `git clone` then ⌘R just works. That is what the empty
// SIPAI_TEAM below is for. A team id hardcoded here would fail on every
// machine that is not a member of it, and an identity NAME hardcoded
// here would fail on every machine without that certificate; those are
// the two failures this file exists to remove.
//
// To sign as yourself WITHOUT touching the project file, drop a
// `Local.xcconfig` beside this one. It is gitignored, and it is
// included LAST, so whatever it sets wins. Two shapes work:
//
//   * You have an Apple developer account — one line is enough, and
//     the identity follows from it:
//
//         SIPAI_TEAM = ABCDE12345
//
//   * You have no account — make a self-signed Code Signing identity
//     (Keychain Access ▸ Certificate Assistant ▸ Create a Certificate,
//     type Code Signing) and name it:
//
//         CODE_SIGN_STYLE = Manual
//         CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY = Your Identity Name
//
// Selecting a team in Xcode's Signing & Capabilities pane works too,
// but that writes the team into project.pbxproj, which is exactly what
// breaks the clone-and-build promise for everyone else.
//
// Worth doing if you run the app rather than just build it: macOS
// identifies an ad-hoc build by the exact bytes of its binary, so every
// rebuild looks like a different app and asks again for access to
// Desktop, Documents and Downloads. EITHER identity above gives the
// build a stable designated requirement — a certificate leaf instead of
// a per-build cdhash — and the grant sticks across rebuilds.
//
// Release builds read none of this — `Release/release.sh` passes the
// Developer ID identity and team on the xcodebuild command line, where
// they override both this file and the project.

SIPAI_TEAM =

DEVELOPMENT_TEAM = $(SIPAI_TEAM)
CODE_SIGN_STYLE = Automatic

// The identity has to follow the team, or setting only SIPAI_TEAM
// leaves this at "-" and hands back an ad-hoc build that looks signed.
// With no team, `$(SIPAI_TEAM:identifier)` is empty, so this resolves to
// SIPAI_IDENTITY_ below — ad hoc. With a team it resolves to an
// undefined variable, i.e. empty, and an empty identity under automatic
// signing is what makes Xcode pick the right certificate itself.
SIPAI_IDENTITY_ = -
CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY = $(SIPAI_IDENTITY_$(SIPAI_TEAM:identifier))

// LAST on purpose. In an xcconfig the last assignment wins, so an
// include placed above these lines lets Local.xcconfig carry SIPAI_TEAM
// and nothing else — every other setting in it is silently overridden,
// and a machine relying on a named certificate signs ad hoc again with
// nothing saying so. Placed here, a `$(SIPAI_TEAM)` set in the included
// file still reaches DEVELOPMENT_TEAM: a reference resolves when the
// setting is evaluated, not where it is assigned.
#include? "Local.xcconfig"
